!! The clip's kept set, and the history that makes it undoable, as a data module.
!!
!! The pane keeps the recording, the picture and the gestures; this module keeps the *decision* — which records the reader has kept, and how to get back to a set they had before. It is the shape the plan called for: a data module that tells the picture what to draw on every change, **one message per cut** and nothing at a pointer rate, which is what makes the round trip cost nothing that matters. A cut is *not* answered from the recording: it narrows **what is on screen**, which is the behaviour Graham asked for on 2026-10-07 — *"the second clip took the same time as the first"* was the symptom of the opposite — and `plotview-check` still pins that from the pane's side, where the filter lives.
!!
!! **What crosses, and in which direction.** The pane sends one dict per request: `what` (`cut`, `undo`, or `run` for a new recording), `tail` for a cut — 0 the whole recording, 1 the window, 2 the two parts outside it — the selection's two ends in steps, and `run`, the recording's last step, which only the pane can measure because the pane is what reads the recording. This module answers with one dict of its own: the kept ranges as the two json arrays the pane's filter indexes (`from`, `to`), how many are live, whether the whole recording stands, what the last cut kept, and the sentence a cut that kept nothing leaves behind. It is addressed to `sender`, which is how a reply has to go: a module may send to `parent`, to `sender`, or to a module it declares itself, and the editor is what relayed the request, so the editor is what relays the answer back to the pane.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The kept list is ranges of steps, never records.** A range is two numbers however big the recording is, so the history — a list of lists of ranges — is a handful of bytes, and thirty of them cost nothing; a saved *set* of records would be a copy of the recording. The pane's filter is what turns ranges back into records, once per picture it draws.
!!
!! §2 **Every number that arrives has to be a number again, because a message carries text.** AllSpeak holds a json value as its text and compares two texts lexically — `9 is not less than 10` — so a cut would keep the wrong records. `add 0 to` is the conversion the filter makes per record, made here once per request.
!!
!! §3 **A cut that keeps nothing is answered like any other, and it is the reason the reply exists at all.** The old path wrote the sentence into the pane's own element and returned; from a module the sentence has to travel, and `changed` is what tells the pane that this answer carries no new set — so the picture is left standing while the sentence is still shown.
    script VizClip
!   -- the boundary's own state --
    variable ClipIn          ! the dict the editor relayed, as it arrived
    variable ClipWhat        ! which of the three it is
    variable ClipChanged     ! whether the reply carries a new set or only a sentence
    variable ClipNotice      ! the sentence a cut that kept nothing leaves behind
    variable ClipOut         ! the dict this module sends back: the set, its counts and the notice
    variable ClipRangeText   ! the kept ranges as the text the pane reads, `10-20` or `0-29;41-100`
    variable ClipSelFrom     ! the selection's two ends, as the request carried them
    variable ClipSelTo
    variable ClipRunTo       ! the recording's own last step, which only the pane can measure
!   -- the kept list, and the history behind it --
    variable ClipKeepFrom      ! array: each kept range's first step
    variable ClipKeepTo        ! array: each kept range's last step
    variable ClipKeepN         ! how many of them are live
    variable ClipKeepFrom2     ! array: the list being built by a cut, before it replaces the live one
    variable ClipKeepTo2
    variable ClipKeepN2
    variable ClipKeepI         ! scratch: the index over the ranges
    variable ClipKeepA         ! scratch: one range's start, while it is being rewritten
    variable ClipKeepB
    variable ClipKeepTail      ! what a cut keeps: 0 the whole recording, 1 the window, 2 the two parts outside it
    variable ClipKeepWhole
    variable ClipKeepSet       ! and what the last cut kept, which is what the notice names
    variable ClipRangeFrom     ! scratch: one range, on its way into the list being built
    variable ClipRangeTo
    variable ClipKeepCap       ! how many ranges the holders hold: past it a splitting cut keeps less
    variable ClipHist           ! array: the kept list before each cut, as text
    variable ClipHistN
    variable ClipHistAt
    variable ClipHistI          ! scratch over it
    variable ClipHistText       ! scratch: the list as text, and the piece being read back
    variable ClipHistPiece
    variable ClipHistParts     ! scratch: the list split on `;` by the loader
    variable ClipHistPair      ! scratch: one range split on `-` by it
    put 16 into ClipKeepCap        ! the pane's figure, restated here so a request without a cap still cuts

    on message go to ClipMessage
    release parent
    stop

!! @hash ed88d1ca
!! @verified 1499a8ea
!!!
!! The boundary: one message in, one message out, and nothing else in this module knows about either.
!!
!! The dispatch is on `what`, and every target ends the turn, because a module's handler is entered by the runtime rather than called: there is no caller to return to. The reply is composed in one place so that the pane's copy of the set and the sentence beside it can never disagree — and **the opening note has the reason** the set travels as ranges rather than records.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **A cut that keeps nothing is answered like any other, and it is the reason the reply exists at all.** The old path wrote the sentence into the pane's own element and returned; from a module a sentence has to travel, and `changed` is what tells the pane that this answer carries no new set — so the picture is left standing while the sentence is still shown.
ClipMessage:
    put the message into ClipIn
    ! **A request changes the set unless it says otherwise**, and the one path that does not — a cut that kept
    ! nothing — sets this back to 0 itself.
    put 1 into ClipChanged
    put property `what` of ClipIn into ClipWhat
    if ClipWhat is `cut` go to ClipCut
    if ClipWhat is `undo` go to ClipUndo
    if ClipWhat is `run` go to ClipNewRun
    stop

ClipNewRun:
    ! **A new recording takes the history with it**: those ranges are steps in a run that is no longer on screen, so an undo into them would cut the picture to a set nobody asked for. The pane reaches the same conclusion for its own copy of the set; this is the half that owns the history.
    put 0 into ClipHistN
    put 0 into ClipHistAt
    put 0 into ClipKeepSet
    go to ClipCutWhole

ClipWire:
    ! The live list, as the text the pane reads, in the history's own form — built the way `ClipHistPush`
    ! builds it, so a range set has one spelling in this file.
    put `` into ClipRangeText
    put 0 into ClipKeepI
    while ClipKeepI is less than ClipKeepN
    begin
        if ClipRangeText is not empty put ClipRangeText cat `;` into ClipRangeText
        index ClipKeepFrom to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepFrom into ClipRangeFrom
        put ClipRangeText cat ClipRangeFrom into ClipRangeText
        put ClipRangeText cat `-` into ClipRangeText
        index ClipKeepTo to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepTo into ClipRangeTo
        put ClipRangeText cat ClipRangeTo into ClipRangeText
        add 1 to ClipKeepI
    end
    return

ClipReply:
    ! **The set travels as one text**, in the same form the history keeps: `10-20`, or `0-29;41-100`. It is not
    ! sent as the two json arrays the arithmetic works on, because **a json holder crosses a property as one of
    ! its slots rather than as the set** — measured 2026-10-13, the day this module was written, when
    ! `tools/clip-check.js` read back `from: 10` for a list holding one range and would have read the wrong
    ! thing for two. The encoding is the history's own, so the file has one spelling of a range set, not two.
    gosub to ClipWire
    put `{}` into ClipOut
    set property `what` of ClipOut to `kept`
    set property `changed` of ClipOut to ClipChanged
    set property `ranges` of ClipOut to ClipRangeText
    set property `n` of ClipOut to ClipKeepN
    set property `whole` of ClipOut to ClipKeepWhole
    set property `tail` of ClipOut to ClipKeepSet
    set property `notice` of ClipOut to ClipNotice
    send ClipOut to sender
    stop

!! @hash 81bb6b76
!! @verified 3ff7e64c
!!!
!! The cut: what a range of steps keeps, and the history that makes it undoable.
!!
!! Every entry here is the note that used to sit in the pane's own code block, carried over with its marker: a cut is taken from **what is on screen** and not from the recording, so the arithmetic is about ranges of steps alone. The marker's number is this block's.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **`add 0 to` the four numbers before any arithmetic on them.** A step read out of a parsed recording is *text*, and text compares lexically — an unconverted `10` is less than `9` — so the ranges this makes would cut the wrong records: measured in isolation before the filter was written, `10 is less than 9` is 1 without the conversion and 0 with it. This is where the selection, the remembered recording end and the range arithmetic all meet, so it is where the conversion belongs.
!! §2 **The one operation the controls share: rewrite the list to what the reader chose to keep.** `ClipKeepTail` says which part of the window that is — 0 the whole recording, 1 the window itself, 2 the two parts outside it — and the selection (`ClipSelFrom`/`ClipSelTo`) is the window the markers make. Keeping the inside intersects each live range with the window; keeping the outside subtracts it, and a subtraction can split one range into two, which is why the list is a list and why the new one is built in the spare pair before it replaces the live one.
!! §3 **Nothing is rewritten but the list.** The recording is re-read on every draw, so a cut is a handful of numbers and the next draw's filter, and `reset` is the empty list. That also means a cut does not survive leaving the pane — the same deal the window itself makes, and the honest one: a recording is a file, and a set of ranges over it is a way of looking, not a property of the run.
!! §4 **A cut that would keep nothing is refused, and the reader is told why.** Subtracting a selection from a list it already covers leaves nothing, and an empty list means "the whole recording" — so the pane would *widen* at a press, which is the one thing this control must never do. The history entry the cut had already saved goes back, and the notice says what happened rather than leaving a reader to work out which button was ignored. It is the notice and not the status line for the reason the design gives: an explanation of a control belongs where the controls are.
!! §5 The whole recording, which is the empty list — both the starting state and what `reset` restores.
!! §6 **The tag is no longer bumped here.** What it does *not* do is ask for a cut's re-fit: it sets `ClipKeepSet` to 0, and the fit reads that to tell the two apart — a cut re-fits the steps and leaves the lines, while giving the whole recording back fits both, which is what `reset` means. Without the bump a reset left the picture clipped: the filter's cache key did not change, so the kept set was never rebuilt (found by the harness, 2026-10-07).
!! §7 One range into the list being built. Ranges go in in order — a live list is ordered and the two halves of a split are added left first — so the built list is ordered too, which is what lets the axis take its ends from the first and last entries and lets the filter's walk stay a walk.
!! §8 **`ClipKeepCap` is a ceiling and not a promise.** At it, a cut that would split a range further simply keeps less of it than it should — sixteen outer cuts, on one recording, without resetting. Said rather than hidden: an overrun here would be a wrong picture with no sign that anything was dropped.
ClipCut:
    ! **The request, off the message.** The pane owns the gesture and the picture, so the range it means arrives
    ! here as three numbers: what the cut keeps (`tail` — the window, the outside, or the whole recording), the
    ! selection's two ends in steps, and the recording's own last step, which only the pane can measure because
    ! the pane is what reads the recording. §1 has the coercion's own note.
    put property `tail` of ClipIn into ClipKeepTail
    ! **The cap is the pane's, because it is the pane that draws the holders** — it is how many ranges its own
    ! arrays can hold — so it arrives with the request. The default is here as well because an unset cap is
    ! not a big cap: `VizKeepN2 is not less than VizKeepCap` with nothing in it compared false and every cut
    ! kept nothing, which is how `tools/clip-check.js` found it on 2026-10-13, the day the module was written.
    if property `cap` of ClipIn is not empty put property `cap` of ClipIn into ClipKeepCap
    add 0 to ClipKeepCap
    put property `from` of ClipIn into ClipSelFrom
    put property `to` of ClipIn into ClipSelTo
    put property `run` of ClipIn into ClipRunTo
    add 0 to ClipSelFrom ! §1
    add 0 to ClipSelTo
    add 0 to ClipRunTo
    if ClipKeepTail is 0 go to ClipCutWhole ! §2–§3
    ! A cut filters the set on screen; only the whole-recording path and an undo read the recording.
    put 0 into ClipKeepWhole
    ! The list the cut replaces, so `undo` can put it back, and the tag, which is what makes a cut
    ! *visible* to the two places that decide a picture.
    put ClipKeepTail into ClipKeepSet
    gosub to ClipHistPush
    put 0 into ClipKeepN2
    put 0 into ClipKeepI
    ! With nothing kept yet, the window *is* the answer — no range to intersect.
    if ClipKeepN is 0 and ClipKeepTail is 1
    begin
        put ClipSelFrom into ClipRangeFrom
        put ClipSelTo into ClipRangeTo
        gosub to ClipCutAdd
    end
    ! And with nothing kept yet, the outside is the two ends of the recording. `0` is where the fit starts,
    ! and `ClipRunTo` is the recording's own last step, remembered in pass one while the list is still empty.
    if ClipKeepN is 0 and ClipKeepTail is 2
    begin
        put 0 into ClipRangeFrom
        take 1 from ClipSelFrom giving ClipRangeTo
        if ClipRangeTo is not less than 0 gosub to ClipCutAdd
        add 1 to ClipSelTo giving ClipRangeFrom
        put ClipRunTo into ClipRangeTo
        if ClipRangeFrom is not greater than ClipRangeTo gosub to ClipCutAdd
    end
    ! Otherwise every live range is rewritten.
    while ClipKeepI is less than ClipKeepN
    begin
        index ClipKeepFrom to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepFrom into ClipKeepA
        index ClipKeepTo to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepTo into ClipKeepB
        if ClipKeepTail is 1
        begin
            if ClipSelFrom is greater than ClipKeepA put ClipSelFrom into ClipKeepA
            if ClipSelTo is less than ClipKeepB put ClipSelTo into ClipKeepB
            if ClipKeepA is not greater than ClipKeepB
            begin
                put ClipKeepA into ClipRangeFrom
                put ClipKeepB into ClipRangeTo
                gosub to ClipCutAdd
            end
        end
        if ClipKeepTail is 2
        begin
            ! What is left of this range on each side of the window.
            if ClipKeepA is less than ClipSelFrom
            begin
                put ClipKeepA into ClipRangeFrom
                put ClipSelFrom into ClipRangeTo
                take 1 from ClipRangeTo
                ! **And it cannot reach past the range it came from.** Without this the piece ran to the
                ! window's edge however far short of it the range ended, so keeping the *outside* of a window
                ! beyond a range *widened* that range: a 10-20 range against a 30-40 window gave `10-29`.
                ! Found 2026-10-13 by `tools/clip-check.js`, in both copies of this arithmetic.
                if ClipRangeTo is greater than ClipKeepB put ClipKeepB into ClipRangeTo
                if ClipRangeFrom is not greater than ClipRangeTo gosub to ClipCutAdd
            end
            if ClipKeepB is greater than ClipSelTo
            begin
                put ClipSelTo into ClipRangeFrom
                add 1 to ClipRangeFrom
                ! The mirror of the cap above: a range that starts past the window's far edge keeps its own
                ! start rather than the window's.
                if ClipRangeFrom is less than ClipKeepA put ClipKeepA into ClipRangeFrom
                put ClipKeepB into ClipRangeTo
                if ClipRangeFrom is not greater than ClipRangeTo gosub to ClipCutAdd
            end
        end
        add 1 to ClipKeepI
    end
    if ClipKeepN2 is 0 ! §4
    begin
        take 1 from ClipHistN
        put ClipHistN into ClipHistAt
        put 0 into ClipChanged
        put `nothing left to keep — the whole recording stands` into ClipNotice
        gosub to ClipReply
        stop
    end
    gosub to ClipCutSwap
    gosub to ClipReply
    return

ClipCutWhole:
    put 0 into ClipKeepN ! §5–§6
    put `[]` into ClipKeepFrom
    put `[]` into ClipKeepTo
    put 0 into ClipKeepSet
    put 1 into ClipKeepWhole
    put 1 into ClipChanged
    gosub to ClipReply

ClipCutAdd:
    if ClipKeepN2 is not less than ClipKeepCap return ! §7–§8
    index ClipKeepFrom2 to ClipKeepN2
    put ClipRangeFrom into ClipKeepFrom2
    index ClipKeepTo2 to ClipKeepN2
    put ClipRangeTo into ClipKeepTo2
    add 1 to ClipKeepN2
    return

ClipCutSwap:
    ! The built list becomes the live one. A loop rather than a swap, because a swap would need a third
    ! pair of holders, and either way this is a handful of numbers.
    put ClipKeepN2 into ClipKeepN
    put 0 into ClipKeepI
    while ClipKeepI is less than ClipKeepN
    begin
        index ClipKeepFrom2 to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepFrom2 into ClipRangeFrom
        index ClipKeepFrom to ClipKeepI
        put ClipRangeFrom into ClipKeepFrom
        index ClipKeepTo2 to ClipKeepI
        put ClipKeepTo2 into ClipRangeTo
        index ClipKeepTo to ClipKeepI
        put ClipRangeTo into ClipKeepTo
        add 1 to ClipKeepI
    end
    return

ClipHistPush:
    ! **The list a cut is about to replace, as one string** — `0-20`, or `0-4;12-20`. The whole history in
    ! a handful of bytes is what makes an undo worth having: a saved *set* would be a copy of the
    ! recording, while this is a few numbers however big the run is, so keeping thirty of them costs
    ! nothing. An empty list is the recording unreduced, which is what undoing past the first cut restores.
    put `` into ClipHistText
    put 0 into ClipHistI
    while ClipHistI is less than ClipKeepN
    begin
        if ClipHistText is not empty put ClipHistText cat `;` into ClipHistText
        index ClipKeepFrom to ClipHistI
        put ClipKeepFrom into ClipHistPiece
        put ClipHistText cat ClipHistPiece into ClipHistText
        put ClipHistText cat `-` into ClipHistText
        index ClipKeepTo to ClipHistI
        put ClipKeepTo into ClipHistPiece
        put ClipHistText cat ClipHistPiece into ClipHistText
        add 1 to ClipHistI
    end
    index ClipHist to ClipHistN
    put ClipHistText into ClipHist
    add 1 to ClipHistN
    put ClipHistN into ClipHistAt
    return

ClipHistLoad:
    ! The text back into the list. Empty text is the recording unreduced — undo, past the first cut, is the
    ! whole run again. `split` fills a slot per piece, so the walk is `index` and `has element`, the same
    ! shape the editor's own buffer walk uses.
    put 0 into ClipKeepN2
    if ClipHistText is empty go to ClipCutSwap
    split ClipHistText by `;` into ClipHistParts
    put 0 into ClipHistI
    while ClipHistParts has element ClipHistI
    begin
        index ClipHistParts to ClipHistI
        put ClipHistParts into ClipHistPiece
        split ClipHistPiece by `-` into ClipHistPair
        index ClipHistPair to 0
        put ClipHistPair into ClipRangeFrom
        index ClipHistPair to 1
        put ClipHistPair into ClipRangeTo
        ! **And back to numbers.** A list read out of the history is *text*, and the next cut's arithmetic on
        ! it would compare lexically — `10 is less than 9` — so an undo followed by a cut would keep the
        ! wrong records. The same conversion the filter makes per record, made here for the same reason.
        add 0 to ClipRangeFrom
        add 0 to ClipRangeTo
        gosub to ClipCutAdd
        add 1 to ClipHistI
    end
    go to ClipCutSwap

ClipUndo:
    ! **Back to the list the last cut replaced**, and it is instant because it is a parse and a draw rather
    ! than a re-filter of the recording: the tag is bumped so the next draw rebuilds the set from the
    ! restored list, which is the same path a cut takes and is why an undo cannot drift from cutting.
    if ClipHistAt is less than 1 stop
    take 1 from ClipHistAt
    index ClipHist to ClipHistAt
    put ClipHist into ClipHistText
    gosub to ClipHistLoad
    ! An undo widens, so it cannot be answered from what is on screen: it reads the recording again.
    put 1 into ClipKeepWhole
    put 1 into ClipChanged
    gosub to ClipReply
!! @hash 4e1c1b0e
!! @verified 613052d7
!!!
